A||N||14||Dan87GT||New V6 has no compression||||1||
Z||000000||Dan87GT||09-30-2001||07:25 AM||||To continue the V6 saga, I still can't get my to V6 to fire, I have replaced the coolant temp sender, ignition module, cap, rotor, check the pick-up coil, double check TDC and timing, fuel pressure ok and it still will not start(getting spark and fuel). So I checked the compression and get nothing, not even a twitch of the needle. Is it possible I tightened the rocker arms to far?? I followed the Haynes manual to a tee(1-1/2 turns after no drag) Any thoughts???I have had this car for 14 years and refuse to take it to a Pontiac dealer.....||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||09-30-2001||08:42 AM||||I would double check the valves to be sure.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ray b||09-30-2001||01:29 PM||||when i set hyd lifters it is done with the motor runing by ear[turn  it intill it stops making a click] how to do that on a car that the covers are blocked by the intake i don't know.<BR>try to set valves so no leaks [leak down rig] or by using your comp gauge turn till comp drops then back till good comp.<BR>remember that hyd lifters pump up some as hot oil gets in to play thats why they are called hyd.<BR>new motor may need to run some to seat rings to get normal comp readings anyway<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000003||pherder||09-30-2001||01:55 PM||||Dan87GT,<P>Yup, you went too far.  <P>Even though the manual state to turn it 1.5 turns after resistance is felt, that is ~ 1 turn too many!!<P>My factory manual for my 350 V8 valve setting is 1/2 turn after resistance is felt on the pushrod.<P>Could there be a typo that has been spread thru out the Fiero manuals?  i.e. it should have been 1/2 turn not 1 1/2 turn all along?<P>I set my 3.2 valves @ 1/2 and haven't had any problems or noise.<P>Paul<P>------------------<BR>Paul Herder<BR>88 Formula - Artero kit car doner and 350 TPI<BR>86 SE - 3.2 w/440T4 Transaxle<BR>95 GMC - 350 TBI - Factory "Custom"<BR>92 Mazda Protege - Stock<BR>55 Jones - First Edition WIFE- all mods, equipment,updates :D<BR>Sedgwick, KS 67135||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||09-30-2001||02:16 PM||||Pherder, you may be right. I've been doing a search on this & the concensus seems to be anywhere from 1/2 to 1 1/2 with most people using less than the 1 1/2. I think I would start with some lesser setting to achieve at least some compression to get it to start and then adjust as necessary. Don't know what it would hurt to leave em a little loose as long as it's not so much the p-rods jump out. That's assuming the correct cam is installed and timing is close enough to begin with. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||David DeVoe||10-02-2001||10:47 AM||||Dan,<BR>This question about valve lash really concerns me as I will shortly be installing heads and valve train in my 2.8. I've checked 2 different manuals and they both say 1 1/2 turns after zero lash. Are they both wrong?? If so it would seem that this error would have affected hundreds perhaps thousands of engine rebuilds and would have been noticed long before this. Let me know what you find out PLEASE!<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dan87GT||10-02-2001||12:54 PM||||Dave,<BR>I am going to give it a try this weekend and I'll post what happens. It is a pain to remove everything to get the valve covers off.||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||10-02-2001||01:03 PM||||rebuilt? did you use total seal rings? they are know for failing before ever working.. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||roadwarrior||10-02-2001||01:25 PM||||one and a half turns after no drag isn't very specific, try getting the specs from you local shop in thousands of an inch and set them with a feeler gauge,also no spark can result from bad wiring a broken wire or two wires grounding themselfs out can stop the engine from runing without afecting the rest of the electrical system, try checking them with a mutli meter or test light.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DreXteR||10-02-2001||02:02 PM||||1Fst2m6 - The total seal are the best rings!(advertised)  How can be fail before working?  I have a big intererst in this, I bougth a set of those rings for my setup and make me a hole in the pocket.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>DreX<P>Damn typos  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by DreXteR (edited 10-02-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by DreXteR (edited 10-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||wantav8||10-02-2001||03:43 PM||||Last year I rebuilt the heads on my 87 V6 and had the same problem, with no compresion. I adjusted the valve down till the pushrod just got a little drag on it then stopped. I have put close to 15000 miles on the car and they are still very quite. I used the used cam and lifters, there was no problem with them pumping up to auto adjust the lash. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||10-02-2001||03:50 PM||||a larger chevy builder and a seperate import builder thought the idea was awsome too but after a few engine built up most failed. 3 of which have been at the shop i deal with. a $12,000 383 in a 94 Camaro died with under 2500 miles on it..  ring failure.. total seal claimed it was the builders fault.. but after a few came in the shop i deal with  and a few others from another reputable builders. both the shops engine builder and the other big name shops have droped that total seal option. they seem ok if your running lots of vacuum and don't drive the car much or lots of boost and rebuild often.. wiseco, or arias or good ol fashin Federal Mogel (sp) many of the TS units are working fine but the ones i've seen are failing...  nope not going in my car. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dan87GT||10-07-2001||08:41 PM||||Thanks for the help fellas, Backed off the valve adjustment 3/4 turns and 150PSI per cylinder!!! But it all back together and she fired right up!! Damn that Haynes manual.....||||reg||1
Z||000013||88formula||10-07-2001||08:53 PM||||Total seal rings are a waste of money! They don't make any more power than regular molly rings. <P>When I adjusted my valves I used a dial indicator to set the lifter preload to .030". It worked out to be 1/2 turn after 0 lash.<P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 10-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Pike||10-07-2001||09:46 PM||||Hi Dan,<BR>I just went through the same exact problem and I replaced all the parts you did and it still would no fire...not even pop. I wish I was there to give you a hand. OK, here is the problem: The stinkin Haynes manual is wrong! Period! You are gonna have to bite the bullet and pull the intake back off and the deck lid (so you have room to work). Dont worry about removing the engine hangers, the valve covers will clear them. You will need to use a 1/4" deep drive socket to get to the screws behind the front and rear hanger. Once you have the valve covers off the engine, place No.1 piston at top dead center at zero degrees (we're not adjusting ignition timeing so forget about 10 degrees advanced etc.)Remember, compression stroke. (No. 1 ready to fire! Now that you've done that, you can now follow the ORDER only from the Haynes book (after this, throw it in the trash!)Now this is how you adjust valve lash on these suckers. First, backup off the rocker nut until you can easily twirl the pushrod in your fingers, then begin tightening down the nut until you can still "JUST" turn the pushrod with your fingers if you press real hard with on it. Your done! go to the next valve in the guide. I was a little better off than you...I read almost 90 lbs. After I re-adjusted mine I read nearly 145 PSI. and the thing fired immediately upon turning the key! Pulling the intake is really a piece of cake...once you make your mind up that your going to have to do it...it's pretty easy!<BR>Good Luck,<BR>Pike||||reg||1
